Each version constant of the schedule cache ends a verbatim history naming the change that
set its current value, so two parallel bumps conflict in git instead of sharing one number.

the scan was handed the schedule cache's source: true
it found the store's version constants, not an empty derivation: true
every version constant's history is well formed and ends on its current value: true
the queued timing objective's generation is one of them: true
no spelling of the queued key hard-codes its generation as a literal queued-v<N>: true
a history whose last entry is the constant's value passes, a gap included: true
a retired value cited in a parenthetical passes: true
a bump without its history line is refused: true
a merge that kept both sides' lines for one value is refused: true
an entry citing no issue is refused, and so is a template's gh-ocannl-<N> placeholder: true
an entry wrapped onto a continuation line is refused: true
a blank line between the block and the constant, or no block, is refused: true
a computed or annotated constant is refused rather than skipped: true
a constant declared twice is refused: true
a header naming another constant does not open this one's block: true
